  "title": "A cancel during the parking handoff must stay terminal",
  "file": "src/pipeline/engine.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "A user cancels a delivery just as it starts waiting for GitHub. The cancellation is accepted, but the factory later resumes and publishes the pull request anyway.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun test \"$TMPDIR/limitless-753-adjudication.test.ts\" --test-name-pattern 'Adjudication F[12]'",
    "output": "F1 after handoff {\"activeAtCancel\":true,\"accepted\":true,\"status\":\"queued\",\"error\":\"cancelled by adjudicator\"}\nF1 after due {\"status\":\"succeeded\",\"error\":null,\"creates\":4}\nF1 after restart {\"status\":\"succeeded\"}\n(pass) Adjudication F1: subscriber cancel at park handoff"
   },
   "evidence": "The temporary test reused the HEAD delivery fixture and cancelled synchronously from a store subscriber observing the parking update. engine.ts:365 checks the cancellation marker before updateRun, but line 368 publishes queued while execution remains active. scheduler.ts:151-153 then handles cancellation with only `active.controller.abort()`. Its completion handler merely executes `this.active.delete(run.id)` and ticks again (138-140); neither queue admission nor executeRun checks the recorded cancellation before engine.ts:218 clears it with `error: null`. At base, these scheduler and resume behaviors already existed, including a similar handoff for deploy parking; this change exposes the gap through ordinary GitHub-outage parking."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "NOT_REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "No production cancel source can act in the window the claim describes. Every way to cancel a run (the web UI, MCP, Discord) arrives as a separate I/O event. The time between the 'queued' write and the scheduler releasing the run passes entirely inside one turn of the event loop, with no awaits, so none of those cancels can land there.",
   "reproduction": "unreproduced: the race only appears when a store subscriber calls cancel synchronously during the parking write, as the reviewer's test did. No such subscriber exists in production. The window holds no await or I/O boundary, so a real cancel request (HTTP, MCP, Discord) cannot run inside it.",
   "evidence": "Parking at engine.ts:367-373 runs `if (deps.store.getRun(runId)?.error?.startsWith(\"cancelled by\")) return cancelled(); ... ctx.run = deps.store.updateRun(runId, { status: \"queued\", stage: null }, ctx.state); ctx.log(...); return \"queued\";`, which is fully synchronous. executeScopedRun has no finally block. executeRun returns `processScope.run(... executeScopedRun ...)` directly. The scheduler's `.catch(...).finally(() => { this.active.delete(run.id); ...})` (scheduler.ts:131-141) therefore runs as microtasks right after the write. The only callers of Scheduler.cancel are app.cancelRun, reached from http.ts:588, mcp.ts:189 and discord.ts:411. All three are I/O handlers, i.e. macrotasks, so none can interleave. The store subscribers that react to run messages (scheduler, poller, notifier, discord, feed, http SSE) never call cancel. On resume, the step that clears the error (`updateRun(... error: null ...)` at engine.ts:214) also runs synchronously in the same tick that picks the run, with no window there either. The same theoretical window already existed at base for the ParkedError and InjectedFault 'queued' paths. Fix commit 44c601ee adds finalizeRecordedCancellation in tick, in .finally and in executeRun, which would close the window if it were reachable."
  }
 },
